The '''2023 Port Waikato by-election''' was held on 25 November 2023. The [[by-election]] was triggered by the death of Neil Christensen, who was a candidate for the [[ACT New Zealand|ACT Party]]. Christensen's death occurred after the close of candidate nominations for the [[2023 New Zealand general election]], but before polling day. The by-election was won by National incumbent [[Andrew Bayly]], who has held the [[Port Waikato (New Zealand electorate)|Port Waikato]] seat and its predecessor [[Hunua (New Zealand electorate)|Hunua]] since 2014.

== Background ==
===2023 general election===
Advance voting for the 2023 general election started on 2 October 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |title=About the 2023 General Election |url=https://vote.nz/voting/2023-general-election/about-the-2023-general-election/ |access-date=12 October 2023 |website=Vote NZ |language=en-NZ |archive-date=19 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230119085454/https://vote.nz/voting/2023-general-election/about-the-2023-general-election/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Christensen's death was announced on 9 October 2023, one week into the advanced voting period.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT's Port Waikato candidate, Neil Christensen, dies |url=https://www.waikatotimes.co.nz/a/nz-news/350087891/act-s-port-waikato-candidate--neil-christensen--dies |access-date=12 October 2023 |work=[[Waikato Times]] |date=10 October 2023 |archive-date=16 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231016205842/https://www.waikatotimes.co.nz/a/nz-news/350087891/act-s-port-waikato-candidate--neil-christensen--dies |url-status=live }}</ref> Because New Zealand uses a [[Mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] voting system, Port Waikato constituents continued to cast a [[party vote]] in the general election, but the electorate vote was suspended and any electorate votes already cast were nullified.<ref name="NZH 9 Oct">{{cite news |last1=Cheng |first1=Derek |title=Election 2023: Act candidate Neil Christensen dies, by-election to be held for Port Waikato |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2023-act-candidate-neil-christensen-dies/N2Z4P5MVKBAY5DJ7PDWLW27Y24/ |access-date=9 October 2023 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=9 October 2023 |archive-date=9 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231009031302/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2023-act-candidate-neil-christensen-dies/N2Z4P5MVKBAY5DJ7PDWLW27Y24/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Ensor |first1=Jamie |title=Election 2023: By-election to be held after election after candidate dies |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/10/election-2023-by-election-to-be-held-right-after-election-after-candidate-dies.html |access-date=9 October 2023 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=9 October 2023 |archive-date=9 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231009051448/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/10/election-2023-by-election-to-be-held-right-after-election-after-candidate-dies.html |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Sowman-Lund |first1=Stewart |title=What the Port Waikato byelection means for the next parliament |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/09-10-2023/what-the-port-waikato-byelection-means-for-the-next-parliament |access-date=12 October 2023 |work=[[The Spinoff]] |date=9 October 2023 |archive-date=16 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231016205226/https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/09-10-2023/what-the-port-waikato-byelection-means-for-the-next-parliament |url-status=live }}</ref> It was the first time since the [[1957 New Zealand general election|1957 election]] that an electorate vote had been delayed due to the death of a candidate.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/newspapers/CHP19571205.2.76 |title=Clutha Election for January 18 |work=[[The Press]] |date=5 December 1957 |volume=XCVI |issue=28452 |page=16 |access-date=18 February 2022 |archive-date=18 February 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220218014447/https://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/newspapers/CHP19571205.2.76 |url-status=live }}</ref>

One of the effects of the by-election will be that the winner will add another seat to Parliament beyond those elected at the general election. At the general election, the seat that would have been filled by the Port Waikato winner was instead filled by a list MP. The winner of the by-election will also enter parliament without ejecting any existing MP.<ref name="NZH 9 Oct" />

=== Electorate ===
The Port Waikato electorate was first established in 1996, ahead of the first MMP election. The electorate was abolished in 2008 and reinstated for the 2020 election. It stretches from the [[Manukau Heads]] at the southern entrance to the [[Manukau Harbour]], south past the eponymous settlement of [[Port Waikato]], to Matira and Pepepe, and inland to Mangatāwhiri and the Maramarua Forest. [[Pukekohe]] is the largest population centre in the electorate. Other significant towns include [[Pōkeno]], [[Te Kauwhata]], [[Tuakau]] and [[Waiuku]]. The electorate includes the [[New Zealand Steel]] mill at Glenbrook, and the lower reaches of the [[Waikato River]].<ref name="Electorate profile">{{cite web |date=3 August 2020 |title=Port Waikato electorate profile |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/port-waikato-electorate-profile/ |accessdate=10 October 2023 |publisher=Parliamentary Library |archive-date=12 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231012044033/https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/port-waikato-electorate-profile/ |url-status=live }}{{CC-notice|cc=by4|author=the Parliamentary Library}}</ref>

Between the 2013 and [[2018 New Zealand census|2018 census]]es, the Port Waikato electorate experienced annual average population growth of 3.3%, considerably higher than the annual average rate for New Zealand as a whole (2.1%). The electorate is predominantly European (78.2%) and Maori (19.0%), with the shares of those identifying as Asian (8.8%), and Pacific Peoples (6.1%), somewhat lower than the average  for New Zealand as a whole. Over a quarter (26.3%) of families had a combined income between $100,000 and $150,000 per year – the second-largest among general electorates.<ref name="Electorate profile" />

