== Candidates ==
All candidates had nominated to contest Port Waikato at the 2023 general election were re-nominated, unless they withdrew.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.legislation.govt.nz/act/public/1993/0087/latest/DLM309673.html |title=Electoral Act 1993, section 153E(4) |access-date=11 October 2023 |archive-date=12 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230312205322/https://legislation.govt.nz/act/public/1993/0087/latest/DLM309673.html |url-status=live }}</ref> On 20 October, the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] announced that its candidate, Gwendoline Keel, would not contest the by-election, because the party viewed it as "unwinnable" and sought to focus on building a strong opposition against the newly elected government.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/500630/port-waikato-by-election-labour-won-t-stand-a-candidate-in-unwinnable-by-election |title=Port Waikato by-election: Labour won't stand a candidate in 'unwinnable' by-election |date=20 October 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |access-date=19 October 2023 |archive-date=19 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231019233554/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/500630/port-waikato-by-election-labour-won-t-stand-a-candidate-in-unwinnable-by-election |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]] candidate Karla Buchanan also withdrew her nomination, while ACT did not nominate a replacement candidate.<ref>{{cite web |title=Port Waikato by-election candidates announced |url=https://elections.nz/media-and-news/2023/port-waikato-by-election-candidates-announced/ |website=Electoral Commission |access-date=20 October 2023 |archive-date=20 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231020070840/https://elections.nz/media-and-news/2023/port-waikato-by-election-candidates-announced/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

